  "step": [
    {
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1,
      "name": "Step 1",
      "text": "Installing window casing might seem daunting, but breaking it down makes it manageable. First, you'll need to accurately measure your window opening. This is super important! Step two, determine your 'reveal' – that small, consistent border around the window jamb that the casing will sit against. Now, carefully cut your head casing (the top piece) and side casings to size, making sure to get those miter joints just right. Next up, dry-fit all your pieces. This lets you check for fit and make any small adjustments before things get permanent. Step five, apply glue to your miter joints – this is a pro tip for strong, long-lasting seams – then nail the casing into the window jamb and wall studs. Once everything’s secured, sink those fastener heads and fill any holes or gaps with wood putty for a smooth finish. It's often best to handle this after the window unit itself is stable and secure in the opening. If you’ve got a lot of windows or they’re in an older home, it might be worth connecting with an expert like HH Remodeling LLC. We can help ensure every piece is cut and fitted perfectly, especially if your windows aren’t perfectly square."
    }
  ],
